<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; Sin" cic="CCC 1846-1876" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">To use after class or in group discussion.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What is sin according to the Catechism?</li>
<li>What are the three conditions for a sin to be mortal?</li>
<li>How does mortal sin differ from venial sin?</li>
<li>What are the seven capital vices?</li>
<li>What does it mean that &laquo;where sin abounded, grace abounded all the more&raquo;?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. What sin do you find hardest to admit? Why do you think that is?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Have you ever felt that a sin &laquo;weighed&raquo; on you? What was that experience like?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. How do you imagine the father&rsquo;s embrace in the parable of the prodigal son?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Which capital vice do you think affects you most? How does it show up in your life?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. What could you do this week to move closer to God&rsquo;s mercy?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. The Prodigal Son in First Person</h4>
<p>Read Luke 15:11-32 slowly. Then write the parable as if you were the prodigal son. What did you feel as you walked away? What were you thinking when you decided to return? What was it like to be embraced by your father?</p>

<h4>B. Psalm 51: The Psalm of the Repentant</h4>
<p>Read Psalm 51 (&laquo;Have mercy on me, O God, in your goodness&raquo;). This is the psalm David wrote after his sin with Bathsheba. Underline the phrases that strike you most. Write them on a card and put them where you will see them this week.</p>

<h4>C. Brief Examination of Conscience</h4>
<p>Make an examination of conscience tonight. Ask yourself: How has my relationship with God been today? Did I say &laquo;yes&raquo; to any evil knowing it was evil? Did I say &laquo;yes&raquo; to any good I could have done? End with an act of contrition.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Memory Verse</h3>

<div class="bible-passage">
<p>&laquo;If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness&raquo;</p>
<p class="bible-passage__ref">1 John 1:9</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Prayer for This Week</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>
Lord Jesus,<br />
You who never tire of forgiving,<br />
I sometimes tire of asking for forgiveness.<br />
Help me to recognize my sins<br />
without despair, without hiding.<br />
Give me the confidence of the prodigal son<br />
to return to You, again and again.<br />
I know You wait for me with open arms,<br />
that Your mercy is greater than my sin.<br />
Amen.
</p>
</blockquote>

</section>

</LessonLayout>
